Stop Mbrtowc() from reading past the end of an invalid multibyte string
while rendering it for an error message, so coercions such as
as.complex() of hostile input stop crashing.

Mbrtowc() (src/main/util.c, exported as Rf_mbrtowc) builds a readable
rendering of an invalid string one character at a time.  The loop passes
mbrtowc() the caller's fixed count -- isBlankString() and the other
callers pass R_MB_CUR_MAX, not strlen(s) -- and decrements it in step
with the read pointer.  Once the count and pointer fall out of step the
count underflows to a huge size_t, so mbrtowc() is told far more bytes
remain than actually do and glibc's UTF-8 converter reads past the end of
the string:

    ==4251==ERROR: AddressSanitizer: SEGV on unknown address (wild-addr-read)
        #0 utf8_internal_loop_single       (glibc iconv)
        #2 __mbrtowc                        (glibc)
        #3 Rf_mbrtowc                      util.c:1627
        #4 Rf_isBlankString               util.c:453
        #5 Rf_ComplexFromString           coerce.c:281

oss-fuzz testcase 5931425707393024 reaches this through as.complex() in a
UTF-8 locale, and 5487840193216512 through type.convert() (ruleout_types()
calls isBlankString() on what Strtod() left unparsed).  The fault needs the
string to end near a heap page, so it is not deterministic (ClusterFuzz
marks both testcases one-time crashers); the over-read -- passing
mbrtowc() a count larger than the string -- is present on every call.

Bound each mbrtowc() call by the bytes actually left before the
terminating nul, tracked in a dedicated counter that cannot underflow.

Found by fuzzing R with libFuzzer + AddressSanitizer (coerce target).

Upstream status: r-devel/r-svn PR #337; R Bugzilla bug 19165 (attachment
3843).
Delete this patch once the fix lands in R trunk (ossfuzz.sh will report it
as already-applied in the meantime, which is harmless).

diff --git a/src/main/util.c b/src/main/util.c
index e9f852fe03..885ac78410 100644
--- a/src/main/util.c
+++ b/src/main/util.c
@@ -1622,19 +1622,26 @@ size_t Mbrtowc(wchar_t *wc, const char *s, size_t n, mbstate_t *ps)
 	size_t sz = 4*strlen(s) + 1;
 	char err[sz], *q;
 	const char *p;
+	/* Bound each mbrtowc() by the bytes actually left before the
+	   terminating nul.  The previous code passed the caller's fixed
+	   count 'n' (typically R_MB_CUR_MAX) and decremented it in step with
+	   'p'; once it fell out of step it underflowed to a huge size_t, so
+	   mbrtowc() was told far more bytes were available than remained and
+	   could read past the end of 's' into unmapped memory. */
+	size_t nb = strlen(s);
 	for(p = s, q = err; *p; ) {
 	    /* don't do the first to keep ps state straight */
-	    if(p > s) used = mbrtowc(NULL, p, n, ps);
+	    if(p > s) used = mbrtowc(NULL, p, nb, ps);
 	    if(used == 0) break;
 	    else if((int) used > 0) {
 		memcpy(q, p, used);
 		p += used;
 		q += used; sz -= used;
-		n -= used;
+		nb -= used;
 	    } else {
 		snprintf(q, sz, "<%02x>", (unsigned char) *p++);
 		q += 4; sz -= 4;
-		n--;
+		nb--;
 	    }
 	}
 	*q = '\0';
